\par\par \b \uc2 Traders Say  \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2014\par 13:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1201 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]-- \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be at it again. According to London-based traders, it has been selling U.S. dollars to prop up the slipping naira. The naira has fallen more than 10% against the buck this year according to Factset data, spurring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into action several times over the last weeks. Oil and natural gas make up almost all of Nigeria's exports and 80% of government revenue, according to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 . Brent crude hit a fresh five-year low late Wednesday. (josie.cox@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 1155 GMT Following Hungary's sharp 0.7% annual fall in consumer prices in November, Citi sharply reduces its inflation forecasts. Now it projects that Hungary's headline inflation will remain in negative territory until 2Q 2015 and accelerate to 1.8% by end-2015. "The monetary policy council may add some dovish tone to its statement in December but will likely wait for further evidence of slowing core prices and certainty about the ECB's quantitative easing before delivering additional rate cuts," Ms. Gargyan says. Core-price moderation and falling oil prices increase the odds of additional minor rate cuts early 2015 and may postpone the beginning of rate hikes to later in 2016, she adds. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1155 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--BNP Paris looks for a solid November U.S. retail sales report as consumers deploy cash saved thanks to lower gasoline prices. The bank says strong data should put a floor under front-end yields, and allow the USD to regain some upside momentum into next week's FOMC meeting. BNPP forecast for November U.S. retail sales is +0.5% m-m, +0.6% ex gas, the DowJones survey forecast is +0.4%, data out 1330 GMT. gary.stride@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1143 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Capital Economics expects the Bank of Russia to raise rates by at least another 100bp in the first half of 2015. "With the ruble likely to remain under pressure and inflation set to rise further over the coming months, we think further rate hikes are likely," it says. alexander.kolyandr@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1142 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Bank of Russia may raise its key rate by another 100 bps by the end of January, as the current similar rate was "below the market expectations," says Daria Isakova from Otkritie brokerage. Notes that by increasing the rate by only 100 bps Thursday and not mentioning a possibility of any cuts in the future in its statement the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "obviously wants to prolong the rate rising." Expects the bank to further squeeze ruble liquidity in the next two weeks, which would put pressure on the banks. Doesn't see capital control measures as a possibility in the next 12 months. (alexander.kolyandr@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:42 EST - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' latest meeting minutes show signs of confidence in future economic policy, saying it expects government savings to increase next year--something which should help tame inflation. The minutes also say improved fiscal behavior in Brasilia could end up reducing the cost of capital, a "stimulus for investment in the medium and long term." Many have been anticipating somewhat of a course change by the Rousseff administration in the wake of her slight re-election win and a bolstered opposition.(paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1133 GMT Ireland's economy slowed sharply in the third quarter, not a huge surprise given the break-neck pace of its expansion in the first half, but still a reminder that no member of the eurozone can long defy the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area's overall fate. GDP rose by just 0.1% on the quarter--below the eurozone average--and 3.5% on the year. In the second quarter, it grew 1.5%. Investment and government spending declined, while net trade was a drag on growth, and household spending was flat. Inflation figures also released Thursday showed the annual rate of inflation slowed to 0.1% in November. Ireland is now looking more like just another eurozone member than some strange wanderer from southeast Asia. (paul.hannon@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @PaulHannon29) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1125 GMT The approved 2015 Czech government budget "shows there is a potential of additional savings or reallocation of expenditures of CZK10 billion," says Citi's economist Jaromir Sindel. The savings may be used to lower the deficit from the currently expected 2.2% of GDP in 2015 closer to 1.8% seen in 2014, Sindel says. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  will likely use most of its planned debt issuance next year to "rollover maturing bonds of CZK133 billion" and finance debt "buybacks of around CZK20 billion for 2016," Sindel says. (leos.rousek@wsj.com, @LeoRousek) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:22 EST - The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Brazil\f28 \uc2 \f2  moderated its hopes for an economic recovery next year with the addition of three words--"second half of"--to its latest meeting minutes. "Activity will tend to enter a trajectory of recovery in the second half of the next year." The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  noted a rising likelihood of fiscal tightening, along with recent rate hikes, could stymie short-term growth. (paul.kiernan@wsj.com; @pkwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:14 EST - Canadian exporters are feeling a bit less bullish about near-term prospects amid instability in the Middle East and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility, says a semiannual report from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Export Development Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the country's trade bank. Its trade-confidence index fell 1.4 points to 75.8 after three consecutive increases which began in spring 2013. Exporters are now less optimistic about domestic sales, global economic conditions and international business. "It's a strange turn of events," says EDC chief economist Peter Hall. "Exporters are expecting stronger sales, but unsettling international developments have tempered their enthusiasm." (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:12 EST - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has a cautious perspective toward future interest-rate hikes, the minutes of its latest meeting show, despite a recent deterioration in the short-term inflationary outlook. "Inflation will tend to remain high in 2015, but will still enter a long period of decline next year," it predicts. Policy makers boosted the Selic a half-point last week and suggested most of the heavy lifting has been done. "Considering the cumulative and delayed effects of monetary policy, among other factors, the committee evaluates that additional monetary-policy efforts will tend to be implemented with parsimony," the minutes say. (paul.kiernan@wsj.com; @dowjonesrio)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1109 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The Polish economy will remain in deflation at least until June 2015 due to low oil prices, gradual zloty strengthening, low unit labor costs and continued demand gap, BZ WBK writes in a note to clients. Adds the monetary council is likely to cut interest rates once more in the first quarter of 2015 bringing the benchmark rate to 1.75% from the current 2% due to deflation, expected temporary slowdown in growth and zloty strengthening. EUR/PLN is expected to gradually drop to 4.12, the bank adds.(patryk.wasilewski@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 11, 2014 07:01 ET (12:01 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141211eacb001mv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
