\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-INTERVIEW-Nigeria must adjust to "permanent" oil price shock-Finance Minister\b0\par\par\uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2014\par 09:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 (Corrects spelling of finance minister's name throughout)\par \par \uc2 By Karin Strohecker\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 20 (Reuters) - Nigeria will have to adjust to oil prices heading lower for some time to come, Finance Minister Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala said on Thursday in an interview in which she also cut the outlook for economic growth next year.\par \par \uc2 Africa's largest economy relies on oil exports for 70 percent of government revenues and has been hit hard as crude prices have fallen to four-year lows below $80 a barrel after trading above $115 as recently as June.\par \par \uc2 Asked if she thought the 30 percent drop in crude prices since June might prove temporary, Okonjo-Iweala said Nigeria needed to adjust to lower levels for good.\par \par \uc2 "The IMF, the analysts feel that the trajectory is going down, and that it is a permanent shock, and we have to adjust (to the possibility) that it is a permanent shock," Okonjo-Iweala told Reuters during the interview in London.\par \par \uc2 "We will have to take the necessary measures and we have already started to do that."\par \par \uc2 She pointed to plans to increase taxes on luxury items and to ban non-essential government travel as first steps, adding that she was also working on plugging tax holes overall.\par \par \uc2 Further down the line, there is more potential to raise revenues through taxation if required, she said.\par \par \uc2 "We have the lowest VAT in the world at 5 percent, so we have a lot of room to manoeuvre," she said, adding there were currently no plans to raise value-added tax in the works.\par \par \uc2 MULTIPLE CHALLENGES\par \par \uc2 Nigeria has been hard hit on several fronts.\par \par \uc2 Along with a worsening domestic picture - attacks by \uc2 Boko Haram\uc2  Islamist militants are almost a daily occurrence - the oil price collapse has fuelled a 20 percent plunge in the local stock market since the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead to economic growth rates next year, Okonjo-Iweala trimmed her outlook to around 6 percent for 2015, down from a forecast of 6.75 percent she made in October.\par \par \uc2 The recent turmoil has also weighed heavily on the naira . It has lost almost 10 percent against the dollar since the start of the year and hit fresh record lows on Thursday, despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have depleted \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/huf76v"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/huf76v }}}\par \par \uc2 Okonjo-Iweala acknowledged the pressure on the naira from falling oil prices and said she expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take action at its monetary policy meeting next week, though she gave no indication of what she expected to see.\par \par \uc2 "I can tell you that we have got the tools with which to manage this," she said. "You will see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  taking the appropriate monetary policy steps to assure we have a soft and not a hard landing."\par \par \uc2 Asked about borrowing plans for next year, she said Nigeria had no plans to return to the Eurobond market but was looking at tapping into the country's large diaspora.\par \par \uc2 Nigeria raised $1 billion in a Eurobond issue last year to increase its total foreign debt. (Editing by Gareth Jones)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-24T08:22:55.000Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141121eabl008hm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
