\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Naira falls again as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  fails to turn investor tide\b0\par\par\uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2014\par 17:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, stock index, background)\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Nov 18 (Reuters) - The naira slid to a new low on Tuesday, as investors worried about the impact of falling oil prices on \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fragile finances shrugged off increasingly \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It hit 173.35 against the dollar, taking its declines for the year to 8.4 percent, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's now regular daily offer of $3 million dollars to each of the country's 21 commercial banks.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, some lenders eschewed the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a bid to curb speculation, restricted the margins they can make from their customers when selling the dollars on.\par \par \uc2 In Tuesday's auction the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market through all of last week - removed the margin cap, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The naira's decline, which has been extended since Finance Minister Ngozi Okojo-Iweala on Sunday cut state revenue estimates for 2015 by 6 percent following sharp declines in the price of Nigeria's main export oil, also hit other local markets.\par \par \uc2 Borrowing costs on Nigerian government bonds rose sharply, with the benchmark 10-year yield up 38 basis points at 13.90 percent, while the main share index lost 1.67 percent.\par \par \uc2 Commenting on the sell-off in bonds, one dealer said: "Investors are worrying about the naira, and the possibility of a rate hike at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next meeting."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next meets on interest rates on Nov. 25.\par \par \uc2 Okonjo-Iweala rejected calls for the government to print more naira to counter the effects of falling oil but said complementary monetary policy measures would be announced by the authorities soon.\par \par \uc2 According to figures on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's website, it has this year spent an average of $26.6 million a day defending the naira, which has tracked falls in other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - notably those in economies that are particularly sensitive to changes in the oil price.\par \par \uc2 As of Nov. 11, the bank's liquid reserves had fallen by $5.77 billion, or 15.7 percent, so far this year to $36.69 billion, the figures show.\par \par \uc2 The naira, meanwhile, has dropped further below the bank's preferred trading band of 150-160, which it burst out of in May. (Reporting by Oludare Mayowa; Additional reporting and writing by Chijioke Ohuocha; Editing by Bate Felix and John Stonestreet) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-18T17:00:41.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141118eabi00fjz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
