\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Nigeria Steps Up to Try to Halt  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2 's Slide -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Josie Cox in London,Patrick McGroarty in Johannesburg \par \uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2014\par 23:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a record-breaking slide, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in Friday to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the U.S. dollar shot up to more than 172 naira, traders said, marking an all-time low for the Nigerian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The move lower is being driven by concerns that tumbling oil prices would hit Africa's biggest economy hard. Nigeria is Africa's top oil producer.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday in New York, the naira recovered to 166.180 against the dollar, compared with 170.215 naira late Thursday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Analysts are warning of a self-reinforcing slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may face political pressure not to raise rates ahead of February's general election," Capital Economics analyst Jack Allen wrote in a note to clients. "But we think that the threat of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis is likely to trump these concerns."\par \par \uc2 The naira's drop placed Nigeria among the oil-rich nations hardest hit by a production glut that has prompted a 28% drop in Brent crude prices from their 2014 high hit in June. The ruble also has caused headaches for the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Norwegian krone and Canadian dollar also have dropped. Oil and natural gas account for 96% of export revenue in Nigeria and about 80% of government revenue, according to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Attempting to curtail the damage to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Nigeria\uc2  on Thursday sought to temper dollar demand by barring importers of goods, including electronics, generators and telecommunications equipment, from procuring dollars at its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions.\par \par \uc2 The bank also limited the amount of money lenders can deposit in the bank's Standing Deposit Facility to 7.5 billion naira, or about $45 million. That regulation should boost the amount of naira between banks, traders said.\par \par \uc2 But Nigeria's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves recently stood at $39.5 billion, much smaller than Russia's $454 billion as of the end of September, limiting Nigeria's ability to hold back the decline.\par \par \uc2 Other corners of Nigerian markets also felt the heat. Nigeria's All-Share Index dropped 3.7% to its lowest point in nearly 18 months, and all three of Nigeria's outstanding dollar-denominated bonds traded marginally lower, according to Tradeweb.\par \par \uc2 The plunge in oil prices could put pressure on Nigeria's budget just as President Goodluck Jonathan and state officials are expected to ramp up spending ahead of national elections set for February.\par \par \uc2 "Investors with a contrarian view on oil may have ordinarily viewed now as an attractive opportunity to buy into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with the added uncertainty around elections, we're not really seeing this," said Razia Khan, head of Africa research at Standard Chartered.\par \par \uc2 A weaker naira could make it more difficult for the government and private investors to fund power, road and rail projects. Officials have said they need to diversify Nigeria's economy and sustain strong growth.\par \par \uc2 The IMF said Nigeria's gross domestic product, which surpassed South Africa as the continent's largest in April, will expand 7% this year after rising 5.4% in 2013.\par \par \uc2 Write to Josie Cox at josie.cox@wsj.com and Patrick McGroarty at patrick.mcgroarty@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCOMM1120141107eab70110n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
