\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-\uc2 Nigeria's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  in the market to defend ailing naira\b0\par\par\uc2 358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2014\par 11:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quote)\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Nov 7 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with dollar sales in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the ailing naira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its deputy governor told Reuters on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Alade\uc2  said the naira, which has lost around 6 percent so far, had been trading outside its preferred band for some time now, but that the bank will continue to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The unit firmed to 169.70 naira at 1023 GMT on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after hitting 173.05 naira in mid-morning trade against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "We would continue to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we have always said that," Alade told Reuters by telephone, adding that the bank was comfortable with level of the country's foreign reserves of around $38 billion.\par \par \uc2 Asked how far the bank was willing to go to defend the naira, she said: "The markets would dictate that."\par \par \uc2 She said that the last time Nigeria devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , oil prices were lower than now, even though the price is declining.\par \par \uc2 "At the last time when did that (devalued), we didn't have the kind of oil prices that we have now, so we are still comfortable," Alade said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come under pressure in the past two months from falling global oil prices, dampening the appetite for assets in Africa's biggest economy and chief oil exporter.\par \par \uc2 The naira has touched new intraday lows nearly every trading session on strong dollar demand partly from foreign investors unnerved by falling oil prices, exiting Nigeria and from domestic importers, worried about the risk of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday restricted the sale of dollars to importers of telecoms equipment, power generators and finished products at its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction, funnelling demand towards the interbank market.\par \par \uc2 The bank also limited lenders and discount houses from placing more than 7.5 billion naira ($44 mln) each as deposits with the regulator, further swelling interbank naira liquidity. (Reporting by Chijioke Ohuocha; Editing by Oludare Mayowa/Jeremy Gaunt) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-7T11:53:36.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141107eab700arm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
