\par\par \b \uc2 Nigerian naira eases as banks cover short dollar positions\b0\par\par\uc2 210 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 17:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LAGOS, March 19 (Reuters) - Nigeria's naira eased against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, driven by strong dollar demand from some lenders trying to cover their short positions on the greenback, dealers said.               \par \par \uc2 The local unit closed at 164.80 naira to the dollar, weaker than Tuesday's close of 164.40 naira.               \par \par \uc2 The local units of two foreign banks sold dollars on Tuesday to boost interbank liquidity, but other lenders bought up hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cover their short positions, dealers said.               \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be comfortable with the naira trading within this range ... many people expect them to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the naira weakens beyond 165," one dealer said.               \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the naira to trade around the 164.50-165.20 level in the short-term, unless oil companies sell large amounts of dollars or the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up its value.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the interbank market to prop up the value of the naira in order to try to keep it trading within its preferred band of 150-160 naira to the U.S. dollar. But it has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-19T17:37:55.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140319ea3j00t3b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pn