\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  pegs look shaky after emerging market rout\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn   \par \uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2014\par 20:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 19 (Reuters) - The devaluation of Kazakhstan's rouble-shadowing tenge has left investors wondering which other closely managed emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be next, with those of commodity exporters like Nigeria and Angola in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 The free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the 'Fragile Five' countries that rely on foreign investment to finance deficits - Brazil, India, Indonesia, Turkey and South Africa - felt the brunt of an emerging market sell-off that began last May.\par \par \uc2 Then, investors who could easily take their money out of these countries returned it to the United States as the prospect of a cut in the Federal Reserve's bond-buying programme pumped up the yields on U.S. bonds.\par \par \uc2 The sell-off has quickened this year as Fed tapering has begun, spreading to countries with higher political risks, such as Hungary and Russia.\par \par \uc2 But oil exporter Kazakhstan's hefty 19 percent devaluation of its tenge earlier this month to keep it in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of regional giant Russia has made investors nervous about less flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged to the dollar or managed within a narrow band had until recently avoided the worst of the sell-off.\par \par \uc2 But as weakness in emerging markets continues, fuelled by worries about a slowdown in China and further monetary tightening in the United States, central banks are likely to run out of firepower to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable.\par \par \uc2 And if they export commodities, for which the price outlook is not comforting, they may need the extra local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that a devaluation would bring, to lower local manufacturing and debt servicing costs.\par \par \uc2 "For the commodity exporters, it's going to be fragile, we are going to see policy mistakes in these countries," said \uc2 Lars Christensen\uc2 , head of emerging markets research at Danske in Copenhagen. "It's time to have a look at some of the African \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 After Turkey raised rates by a whopping 425 basis points at an emergency meeting last month, and other Fragile Five members tightened policy to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , attention has turned to countries such as Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 It was a star performer last year due to growth in financial services and consumer markets, along with its status as Africa's top oil producer.\par \par \uc2 But Nigerian stocks have fallen 6 percent this year after a 47 percent rise last year, and the naira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen below its managed 150-160 band to the dollar despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With presidential elections next year, \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has a political risk problem, while there will also be a change at the helm of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the next few months when highly respected governor Lamido Sanusi steps down.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have dropped by 10 percent from a year ago to $42 billion - barely six months' worth of import cover. While the minimum yardstick for import cover is normally three months, analysts say an oil exporter like Nigeria should have more of a cushion because oil is priced in dollars.\par \par \uc2 "We are at the end-game," said Angus Downie, head of economic research at Ecobank. "There is a real risk if the naira does not stabilise and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot maintain market confidence, then devaluation is what we are looking at."\par \par \uc2 Devaluation would cut the value of international investors' holdings of Nigeria's domestic debt or local shares.\par \par \uc2 Renaissance Capital sees a possible devaluation after the change of governor, to 160-170 naira per dollar, although it and several other analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten monetary policy first to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The smaller, more closed economy of Angola may also suffer a devaluation of its kwanza \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it depends both on oil exports to and investment from a slowing China.\par \par \uc2 The Angolan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable through \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves also stand only at around six months of import cover, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 CHINA SHOCK\par \par \uc2 "The China shock is likely to lead to depreciation pressures on the Angolan kwanza," wrote Danske's Christensen in a recent blog. "If the Angolan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to maintain a quasi-pegged exchange rate then these depreciation pressures will automatically translate into a significant monetary tightening.\par \par \uc2 "The right thing to do is therefore ... to allow the kwanza to depreciate to adjust to the shock."\par \par \uc2 Other oil-exporting markets with fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as Algeria, Kuwait or the United Arab Emirates, are seen at less risk of devaluation.\par \par \uc2 Their markets are less open to the fickle speculative flows that have hit those emerging markets which are more integrated with the global financial system, or they enjoy larger cash buffers with which to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Only further weakness in the growth outlook for China and the United States, impacting commodity demand, could shake these \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  props.\par \par \uc2 "For the GCC (\uc2 Gulf Cooperation Council\uc2 ) countries there is no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk impact," said Slim Feriani, Chief Executive Officer of Advance Emerging Capital.\par \par \uc2 "The oil price has more of an impact. If oil prices were to fall and stay low for three, four, five months, it would start to affect sentiment." (Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-22T19:59:59.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140219ea2j005my\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
