\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Nigerian naira closes down 1.2 pct to weakest in four months\b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2014\par 15:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Cbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fails to stem decline\par \par \uc2 * Lifting \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureau restrictions adds to selling pressure\par \par \uc2 * Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling across the globe (Adds naira close, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Tim Cocks\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Jan 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's naira fell 1.7 percent to its weakest level against the U.S. dollar in four months on Monday, defying \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions to defend it.\par \par \uc2 The naira closed at 162.50 to the dollar, down from 160.50 at the previous day's close. Intraday, it had fallen as low as 163.35 to the dollar before coming back up.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Nigeria\uc2  wants the naira to remain within a trading band of 150-160 naira to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sliding across the board, driven by concerns over China's economic slowdown, increased political risks in Ukraine, Turkey and Thailand and by expectations of further tapering of the U.S. Federal Reserve's bond-buying programme.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the restriction on the amount of dollars banks can sell to bureaux de changes, in a bid to narrow the gap between the official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate and the parallel interbank market.\par \par \uc2 It had limited the amount of dollars that bureau de change firms can buy from banks to $250,000 a week in September in order to curb demand for dollars.\par \par \uc2 So the removal of the limit on Monday put further selling pressure on the naira, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also held its twice weekly auction of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with results expected by 5 p.m. local time (1600 GMT), and in addition sold some extra dollars onto the market to try to lift the local unit.\par \par \uc2 Governor Lamido Sanusi said last Thursday his \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to seek a stable exchange rate for as long as its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves could support it, but he warned Nigeria's oil savings were running out.\par \par \uc2 Sanusi ruled out a devaluation of the naira because it would "not affect the current account balance, given the highly inelastic nature of our imports and the dominance of oil".\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the cash reserve requirement on public sector deposits held by banks in a bid to shore up the naira, reflecting its concern about loose fiscal policy ahead of elections next year.\par \par \uc2 Nigeria faces a hotly contested poll early in 2015. The country's fiscal position usually slips around election time, when spending on patronage to secure seats surges, but this one is expected to be the most closely fought since the end of military rule in 1999. (Reporting by Tim Cocks; Additional reporting by Mayowa Oludare Editing by Jeremy Gaunt)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-1-30T14:55:57.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140127ea1r00afh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
