\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria \highlight\uc2 's naira firms as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2  after US Fed taper\b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2013\par 18:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Naira firms as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells dollars in wake of Fed taper\par \par \uc2 * Nigeria bond yields mixed as U.S. start tapering\par \par \uc2 * Stocks up almost 1 pct on Thursday (closing bond yield, naira, index, quotes and background)\par \par \uc2 By Chijioke Ohuocha\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Dec 19 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the naira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, lifting it against the dollar despite the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to reduce the stimulus that has flooded emerging markets with cash.\par \par \uc2 The naira, which lost 0.53 percent on Thursday morning, firmed against the U.S. dollar to close at 158.55 on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The local unit had ended at a two-month closing low of 159.95 on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Dealers say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold an undisclosed amount of dollars to some lenders on Thursday to support the naira after it weakened to 160.85 naira, its weakest since mid-October.\par \par \uc2 Yields on Nigerian government bonds were mixed as investors digested the Fed news.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year benchmark bond, which is listed in the \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  emerging market government bond index (GBI-EM), closed flat at 13.03 percent, after yields had initially risen 20 basis point in mid-trade to 13.23 percent.\par \par \uc2 The 5-year bond saw its yield rise marginally to 13.1 percent on Thursday, from Wednesday's close of 12.82 percent.\par \par \uc2 "There was a bit of sell-off in the morning (but) markets stabilised with bids coming back," one dealer said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Reserve said late on Wednesday that it would trim the pace of its monthly asset buying by $10 billion to $75 billion, saying the U.S. economy was finally strong enough for it to start tapering.\par \par \uc2 Stocks shrugged off the tapering news to gain 1 percent, lifted by the banking and consumer goods sectors. Africa's second biggest index has gained 40 percent since this year.\par \par \uc2 "Markets should be relieved now that an element of uncertainty around tapering has been removed. I think the Nigerian market will shift its focus to domestic issues including the direction of monetary policy next year ... and general macro-stability in the run up to elections," said Alan Cameron, economist at CSL Stockbrokers.\par \par \uc2 Domestic fund managers have been selling riskier assets and moving money into short-term debt due to uncertainty over government spending ahead of elections in 2015 and the future leadership at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next year. (Additional reporting by Oludare Mayowa; Editing by Tim Cocks and Hugh Lawson)\par \par \uc2 NIGERIA-MARKETS/ (UPDATE 1) | AFN | ABN | D | M | RBN | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020131219e9cj000y5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
