\par\par \b \uc2 Cedi seen easing next week, Kenya shilling up\b0\par\par\uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 14:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Oct 14 (Reuters) - Ghana's cedi and Nigeria's naira are likely to ease slightly next week, while Kenya's shilling should strengthen due to the start of regular year-end dollar inflows.\par \par \uc2 GHANA\par \par \uc2 The cedi <GHS=> is seen easing next week, mainly due to growing corporate demand for the dollar. It closed on Wednesday at 1.4265 to the dollar, easing from the week's support around 1.42.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the coming week's price action to be choppy with potential for the dollar to break higher," said Jacob Brobbey of Barclays Bank said.\par \par \uc2 Biggles Amponsah of Access Bank Ghana said the cedi could settle at around 1.4300 by the middle of next week.\par \par \uc2 "Next week may not be so favourable for the cedi, owing to growing demand for dollars, ....notably from the oil and gas sector," he said.\par \par \uc2 Ghana is on track to become a commercial oil producer by the end of this year. Amponsah said he expected any much further weakening of the cedi to trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 NIGERIA\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The naira <NGN=D1> is likely to ease further next week, shrugging off \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempts to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and calm the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by providing a platform for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards.\par \par \uc2 "There is still much uncertainty in the market, and most importers are buying up dollars to ... hedge against future depreciation of the naira," one dealer said.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at 152.36 to the dollar at 1204 GMT, firming slightly from its 152.47 close on Wednesday. State energy firm NNPC sold about $250 million to banks on Tuesday, some of which were delivered on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 KENYA\par \par \uc2 Kenya's shilling <KES=> is expected to strengthen against the dollar as traditional year-end inflows from the tourism, tea, coffee and horticulture sectors get under way. A strengthening euro would also underpin the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks posted the shilling at 80.40/50 compared to last Thursday's close of 80.55/65.\par \par \uc2 Technical analysis showed support for the dollar at 80.15/20, analysts said. The shilling fell through 80.00 on Aug 12 when it closed at 80.25, a level it has not touched since then.\par \par \uc2 "If the euro continues to gain then we should see the shilling track it," said Kennedy Butiko, deputy head of treasury at \uc2 Bank of Africa\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TANZANIA\par \par \uc2 Tanzania's shilling <TZS=> is likely to remain under pressure because of strong corporate dollar demand.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, commercial banks posted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1,500/1,505 compared with 1,485/1,490 last Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Traders predicted the shilling would trade in the 1,490-1,500 range in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, we expect the weak shilling to remain at current levels," said Eric Chijoriga, a trader at National Bank of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Tanzania\uc2  traded $62.16 million on its Interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market in the week ended Tuesday, according to statistics on its website.\par \par \uc2 UGANDA\par \par \uc2 The Ugandan shilling <UGX=> is seen in a tight range against the dollar, with a slight weakening bias due to anticipated dollar demand from the energy sector, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks posted the unit at 2,259/2,262 compared with last Thursday's close of 2,247/2,252.\par \par \uc2 "We expect demand to pick up next week from the energy sector so we are likely to see some slight depreciation of the shilling. We should see trade in the 2,250-2,265 range," said Faisal Bukenya, head of market-making at Barclays Uganda.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders on Monday agreed to reduce spreads from 10 shillings to 5 shillings because of market illiquidity.\par \par \uc2 "Interbank activity has picked up given the narrowing of the spreads, although real demand is still absent," Bukenya said.\par \par \uc2 Charts suggest dollar resistance around 2,275.\par \par \uc2 "It looks like demand for dollars is building up. We might see a stable shilling next week with demand and supply for dollars evenly matched" said Lucas Ochieng head of treasury at Orient bank.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kwasi Kpodo, Susan Nabadda, Fumbuka Ng'wanakilala, Richard Lough and Oludare Mayowa; Compiled by Ed Cropley; Editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 AFRICA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 /|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|AFN|SF|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101014e6ae00119\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
