\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Nigeria well placed to weather global crisis -cbank\b0\par\par\uc2 By Randy Fabi   \par \uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2008\par 13:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comment from head of \uc2 Oceanic Bank\uc2 , paragraphs 14-15)\par \par \uc2 ABUJA, Oct 3 (Reuters) - Nigeria's financial markets are sufficiently liquid, its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are safe and its banks' balance sheets are strong despite the global credit crisis, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Chukwuma Soludo said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Global market turmoil has led institutional investors to dump perceived risk, dampening enthusiasm for emerging frontier markets like those in sub-Saharan Africa and leading to outflows of foreign capital from countries including Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 "We now begin to have cross-border contagion," Soludo told a conference in the capital Abuja attended by senior Nigerian bankers, government officials and guest speakers including former U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers.\par \par \uc2 "Given the credit crunch in the advanced industrial world, several of the institutional investors from those markets began to pull out of our markets," he said.\par \par \uc2 The 10 trillion naira ($85 billion) Nigerian Stock Exchange <.LAGLG> has fallen by more than a third since early March to its lowest level in more than a year, although the decline comes against the backdrop of a dismal performance by its peers.\par \par \uc2 The emerging stocks benchmark <.MSCIEF> dropped to its lowest level since July 2006 on Friday.\par \par \uc2 But Soludo said the economic fundamentals in the world's eighth biggest oil exporter were sound and said he saw no immediate need for further regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Committee decided at an emergency meeting last month to cut its benchmark interest rate to 9.75 percent from 10.25 percent to provide more liquidity.\par \par \uc2 It also injected about 150 billion naira ($1.3 billion) into sub-Saharan Africa's second biggest economy by reducing the cash reserve requirement to 2 percent from 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We have taken a lot of action in terms of liquidity ... I believe the market is as liquid as it should be at a time like this," he told the meeting.\par \par \uc2 "Our doors are open, we are free and open to lend to the banks, our discount windows are open, our liquidity ratios are down, our monetary policy rate is down, the minimum cash reserve requirement is down," he said.\par \par \uc2 STRONG FUNDAMENTALS\par \par \uc2 What attracted foreign investors to Nigeria in the first place -- the sheer size of its economy meaning its financial markets were relatively liquid -- is also what could shield it from the worst of the global crisis, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 They say its banks should be well capitalised enough to cope with tightening global credit lines, while in the long term its stock market has enough domestic liquidity to shrug off the departure of some foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Cecilia Ibru, head of \uc2 Oceanic Bank\uc2  , one of Nigeria's biggest, said foreign credit lines were still open.\par \par \uc2 "They are still lending to us because we are creating superior value here. We are in a period of growth," she said.\par \par \uc2 Soludo said Nigerian speculators and regulators had been panicked by the global environment, accelerating the stock market decline. But he told Reuters on the sidelines of the meeting that he believed it was bottoming out.\par \par \uc2 He said Nigeria's banks had strong balance sheets, were continuing to post profits, and were not under the sort of pressure seen in developed markets such as the United States.\par \par \uc2 Junior Finance Minister Remi Babalola also told Reuters on Friday that a series of measures to further instil confidence in the Nigerian economy would be announced before the end of the month, including "close collaboration and meetings with banks and companies to ensure that they are not holding risky assets".\par \par \uc2 President Umaru Yar'Adua painted a rosy economic picture in a state address to mark Independence Day on Wednesday, citing average growth of around 6.9 percent, single-digit inflation and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $63 billion.\par \par \uc2 Soludo said core inflation, which excludes volatile food and energy prices, was running at an acceptable 3.9 percent and said Nigeria's foreign reserves were secure, despite fears they might be at risk from bank failures in the West.\par \par \uc2 "Our foreign reserves are safe. We have been very prudent in terms of where we put them, the institutions where they are," Soludo said.\par \par \uc2 (To see "Foreign funds cut Nigeria exposure, impact limited - analysts" click on [ID:nLU225464])\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Nick Tattersall; Editing by Ron Askew)\par \par \uc2 NIGERIA-FINANCIAL/CBANK (UPDATE 4, PICTURE)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AFN|SF|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|G|O|OIL|RNP|DNP|PGE|PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081003e4a3000ti\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
