\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria \highlight\uc2  naira strengthens after  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 226 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2006\par 15:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (updates with details and quotes)  \par \par \uc2  LAGOS, May 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday, selling $118 million in a special auction to support the naira, traders said.   \par \par \uc2  The naira strengthened to 128.65 to the dollar at the interbank market on Friday, reversing Thursday's depreciation which saw it drop to 128.80, they added.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market reacted to the increased supply of dollars immediately after the result of the auction was announced.  \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was meant to support the naira from further depreciation," one dealer said. "The new dollar inflow would keep the naira stable for now."  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold just $64.25 million at its regular Wednesday auction, versus an offer of $120 million, because many bids quoted by participating banks were at rates stronger than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's established band.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the largest supplier of dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in oil exporter Nigeria, because it is recycling dollar-demoninated oil receipts.  \par \par \uc2  Demand for dollars is fuelled by a wide differential between the interbank market and black market, where some banks sell dollars at 143 naira.     \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 NIGERIA\b0 \highlight\uc2