\par\par \b \uc2  FEATURE - New Nigerian ruler faces reform dilemma. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Matthew Tostevin \par \uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 1999\par 13:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LAGOS, May 24 (Reuters) - The man who did more than anyone to entrench state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy in the 1970s takes the presidency on Saturday, to the sound of incessant calls for liberal reform. \par \par \uc2  General Olusegun Obasanjo has little choice but to do something radical, given the economic mess he inherits after more than 15 years of corrupt military misrule in the oil-producing country of at least 108 million. \par \par \uc2  But the former army ruler is pulled in two directions. \par \par \uc2  Foreign creditors holding the key to debt relief want the sale of woefully run state enterprises and the scrapping of subsidies as well as independent monitoring of Nigeria's finances. \par \par \uc2  The feeling of many in the presidential camp is that Obasanjo - a down-to-earth farmer who delights in eating with his fingers - has the personal integrity to make sure state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not misdirected this time round. \par \par \uc2  "Obasanjo is a 'government can fix it' kind of guy," said Pat Utomi, Director of the Lagos Business School. \par \par \uc2  "He means well, believes that government can actually fix those problems, but is going to find out quickly that government does not have enough resources to fix them. That will be the telling point," he told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  INDICATORS OF PAIN \par \par \uc2  Low crude prices and a last minute spending splurge by outgoing General Abdulsalami Abubakar's government, have cut foreign reserves to little more than $3 billion from $7 billion in late 1998. \par \par \uc2  Although some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  went to support the naira, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dropped 20 percent on the open market this year. \par \par \uc2  The stock exchange index is at its lowest since 1995 and down 14 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  "Oil prices have risen since March but even some of the remaining reserves are effectively accounted for by commitments. There is very little to play with," said one official. \par \par \uc2  In a few years, Nigeria may reap a fresh oil and gas bonanza from deep offshore waters far from the volatile Niger Delta region which currently pumps about 1.8 million barrels per day. \par \par \uc2  In the short term, Obasanjo has little more in his favour than foreign goodwill and the fear abroad that economic collapse would end Nigeria's latest attempt at democracy. \par \par \uc2  IMF KEEN TO DEAL \par \par \uc2  Despite Nigeria's failure to meet targets agreed with the International Monetary Fund (IMF) in January, the Fund hopes to negotiate a medium term programme with the new team to pave the way for short term loans and later debt relief. \par \par \uc2  But the creditors who could reschedule some of an estimated $30 billion debt want to see clear evidence that money is not being stolen or squandered. Some fear Obasanjo feels rescheduling is a right Nigeria has already earned. \par \par \uc2  Britain has promised help only if Nigeria agrees to outside monitors in the finance ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Once you owe money, you lose an element of sovereignty. But I want to make it clear, that they cannot tell me what to do in Nigeria," Obasanjo told the debt campaign group Jubilee 2000. \par \par \uc2  PRIVATISATION ESSENTIAL FOR SUPPORT \par \par \uc2  Essential for Nigeria to win much outside support is the privatisation of scores of inefficient state enterprises that leave Nigerians short of fuel, power and communications and absorb an estimated $2 billion a year in subsidies. \par \par \uc2  Many of the firms are relics of an era when Obasanjo's last government - like many in Africa - began borrowing for prestige projects and sowed the seeds of later debt crisis. \par \par \uc2  Potentially the biggest privatisation is a sale of part of the state's average 57 percent stake in oil-producing joint ventures. A study by a U.S. bank suggested the sale of only six percent could bring in more than $2 billion. \par \par \uc2  Powerful vested interests have ensured that previous efforts at privatisation have done little more than create new committees and reams of paperwork headed for nowhere but the files. \par \par \uc2  "Privatisation is essential. We're looking at a policy thrust in which we want to free this economy from the constraints that create opportunities for people to collect economic rent," said Utomi. \par \par \uc2  "Perhaps we could still survive without, but life is not about surviving, it's about growth. We've got 65 percent of the population living below the poverty line. I don't know how that can be called surviving." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv5o00054\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
