\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Nigeria ministries approve spending spree. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Felix Onuah \par \uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1999\par 17:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ABUJA, April 30 (Reuters) - Nigerian ministries plan to spend more than 60 billion naira ($630 million) on fresh projects in the dying days of the military administration, officials said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The new commitments come despite a plunge in foreign reserves to less than $4 billion at the end of March from nearly $7 billion at the end of last year because of increased government spending and low earnings from oil, Nigeria's major export. \par \par \uc2  Government documents obtained by Reuters showed new spending was approved by 10 ministries after a single meeting on April 22. \par \par \uc2  The extra commitments are certain to annoy the incoming administration of President-elect General Olusegun Obasanjo, who is due to take office on May 29. \par \par \uc2  They will also worry donors, who are already concerned at the way foreign reserves have tumbled as the army prepares to bow out after 15 years of corruption-tainted rule in the country of 108 million. \par \par \uc2  "Approval for the spending has been given, although funding has yet to be disbursed," one official told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Among the funds approved after the April meeting were more than $250 million to improve radar coverage, more than $20 million for an aerial survey of Nigeria's mineral reserves, and money for roads, railways, power lines and new computers. \par \par \uc2  The meeting did not discuss the need for extra spending for \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's involvement in the West African ECOMOG \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  force in Sierra Leone and for strengthening security in the restive oil-rich Niger Delta region. \par \par \uc2  "This spending is the tip of an iceberg," said one analyst with close contacts to General Abdulsalami Abubakar's government. "There will be very little money left to play with by the time Obasanjo takes office." \par \par \uc2  Officials of Obasanjo's Peoples Democratic Party (PDP) reacted cautiously to news of the latest expenditure plans on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We're not in a position to ascertain such an amount will be spent and are rather hamstrung on making comments," Aniete Okon, PDP spokesman told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "But if it's true, then it's part of the continuing catalogue of questionable actions taken by the outgoing government," Okon added. \par \par \uc2  Abubakar is hailed at home and abroad for his personal determination to end military rule and embark on liberal economic reforms after assuming power following the death of corrupt dictator Sani Abacha last June. \par \par \uc2  But Abubakar's short-term economic record is clouded at home by severe pressure on the naira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten liquidity and drive interest rates to 35 percent, the highest in more than a decade. \par \par \uc2  Ending 10 years of sour relations, the International Monetary Fund agreed a staff-monitored programme in January with the understanding that it could lead to a medium-term plan and possible debt relief and concessionary funding. \par \par \uc2  But as well as meeting targets on privatisation and financial stability, Nigeria must also prove it has tried to end its record of massive corruption and of spending on wasteful projects. \par \par \uc2  ($1=95 naira) REUTERS DLM. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4u01upb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
