\par\par \b \uc2  Nigeria still hopeful of early deal with IMF. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tunde Obadina \par \uc2 709 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ABUJA, May 22 (Reuter) - Nigeria will later this week begin talks with the IMF which it hopes will produce an early accord to pave way for negotiations with mainly western creditors for debt relief. \par \par \uc2  Nigeria's finance minister Anthony Ani said his government was still aiming to reach an agreement with the IMF and the World Bank on a "home grown" Medium Term Economic Adjustment Programme by the start of the third quarter of 1995. \par \par \uc2  "We are still very optimistic because we are on course," he told Reuters in an interview on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Ani said he was confident that policy differences between Nigeria and the IMF could be iron out at the talks. \par \par \uc2  The start of the talks with a visiting IMF team has been shifted from Monday to May 27 because of the annual meeting of the African Development Bank in Abuja this week. \par \par \uc2  In its 1995 budget Nigeria's military government announced measures that took the country back along the path of free market reforms after a year of turning its back on IMF-backed reforms started by the previous administration in 1986. \par \par \uc2  International finance agencies have commended aspects of the 1995 budget, including the re-introduction of an autonomous \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the removal of laws that restricted inflow of foreign investment. \par \par \uc2  But they say the government needs to further liberalise the economy, including abolishing the official exchange rate of 22 naira to the dollar, a fraction of rates of about 83.50 naira to the dollar available in the autonomous market. \par \par \uc2  They also oppose the retention of a 21 percent ceiling on interest rates and apparent moves to take over privatised banks. \par \par \uc2  Ani said the official exchange rate exists only for internal accounting purposes and that the effective rate in the economy was that of the autonomous market. \par \par \uc2  He said the government aims for a unified rate and hoped to reach an agreement with IMF on a time-table for achieving this. \par \par \uc2  "We hope to come to an agreement when this will cease. We are not going to have two rates for ever. We expect them to merge between now and the next 18 months," he said. \par \par \uc2  Ani said the government was looking at ways to make the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  discount rate the focus of bank interest rates. \par \par \uc2  He added that the government would not allow a return to the 1993 situation when interest rates topped 100 percent. \par \par \uc2  "We cannot allow a situation where banks move interest rates so that they adversely affect manufacturers and the economy." \par \par \uc2  Ani said the government was still committed to the policy of privatisation began by the previous military administration in 1988 and suspended in January this year by the present regime. \par \par \uc2  "We are not going back on the privatisation policies. Government is committed to privatisation," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said government plans to re-establish its presence in the country's four biggest banks, privatised between 1992 and 1993, does not involve re-nationalisation. \par \par \uc2  He said the decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the First Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Union Bank, United Bank for Africa and Afribank was to prevent them being taken over by some people and then made distressed. He said 49 out of Nigeria 120 banks are distressed. \par \par \uc2  "There are maneuvers now by certain individuals to hijack and take over the four banks," he said. \par \par \uc2  Ani said the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the four banks were too crucial to the economy for the government to allow them to be taken over by people who might run them to the ground. \par \par \uc2  He stressed that the state will not take over shares already issued or nationalise holdings of foreign shareholders. \par \par \uc2  The government is believed to be planning to buy unissued shares in these banks and raise their capital. \par \par \uc2  Ani said the government's Medium Term Adjustment Programme is an update of the programme worked out in 1993 by the then head of the Transitional Council, businessman Ernest Shonekan. \par \par \uc2  That three-year programme, which was never implemented, was was geared to achieving a 5.3 percent average real GDP annual growth rate, cut inflation to five percent, reduce fiscal deficit as a percentage of GDP to 3.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr5m07i5c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
