\par\par \b \uc2  Nigeria  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market slow, awaits  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  move. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tunde Obadina \par \uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LAGOS, Feb 16 (Reuter) - Trading remains slow in Nigeria's newly legalised autonomous \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as banks and their clients wait for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and inject scarce funds into the system. \par \par \uc2  "We don't have funds to sell nor do we have buyers. People are anticipating a CBN (\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  anytime from now," a dealer in one of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's leading commercial banks said. \par \par \uc2  The naira now stands at about 82 to the dollar, barely changed from the week's start. \par \par \uc2  "There has been little movement in the market because people are still waiting to see how the new system works," a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at another Lagos-based commercial bank said. \par \par \uc2  Many exporters remain reluctant to repatriate their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  proceeds through authorised banks because they are either awaiting better exchange rates or doubt the durability of the military government's new liberalisation move, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  The government, under pressure from local private sector groups and foreign creditors, legalised the autonomous market in mid-January after a year of imposing an officially set and highly subsidised fixed exchange rate regime. \par \par \uc2  Under the new system, banks and exchange bureaux are free to set market-determined rates. \par \par \uc2  The CBN has stopped its fortnightly \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales to the public, saying it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it deemed necessary. \par \par \uc2  The CBN, whose funds account for the bulk of domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  supplies, has not had a formal sale since mid-December, though it has sold funds to some corporations. \par \par \uc2  Many manufacturers and other importers are waiting for \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before buying \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , assuming a CBN sale would strengthen the naira. \par \par \uc2  Some private sector groups have criticised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for not pumping \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the import-dependent economy until now. "The CBN is just standing by watching. It should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now," Uzor Okeke, director-general of the Manufacturers Association of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAN) told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should aim to strengthen the naira so that local production costs do not rise. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty in the financial sector and delays in the issue of new import tariffs were hampering productive activity, Okeke said. "Companies haven't started importing. They are still using stocks which will run out.It is a dangerous situation," he said. \par \par \uc2  --Lagos newsroom +234 1 2630317 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr2g013ym\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
